# How the price finds its way
subtitle: "How a shortage pushes a market to its new price after demand jumps, and why slow-growing crops can circle that price forever."

## How the price finds its way
domain: {"x":[0,100],"y":[0,100]}
vars: {"s":0}

    axes ax x_label "Crates a day" y_label "Price per crate" hidden true
    curve supply #2f6b8f expr "20 + x" x_from 5 x_to 75 hidden true
    label label_S "S" above-left #2f6b8f attach_to supply hidden true
    curve demand #b5482e expr "80 + s - x" x_from 5 x_to 75 hidden true
    label label_D "D" above-right #b5482e attach_to demand hidden true
    dot eq at.intersection_of ["demand","supply"] hidden true
    label label_eq "E" below-right attach_to eq hidden true
    curve pline dashed #8a5fa8 expr "50" x_from 0 x_to 75 hidden true
    label l_pline "$50" below #8a5fa8 attach_to pline hidden true
    dot qs_pt #2f6b8f at.on supply at.x 30 hidden true
    dot qd_pt #b5482e at.on demand at.x 50 hidden true
    arrow excess qs_pt -> qd_pt #8a5fa8 hidden true
    label l_excess "shortage 20" below #8a5fa8 attach_to excess hidden true
    arrow climb qs_pt -> eq #8a5fa8 hidden true
    label l_qs "30" above-left #2f6b8f attach_to qs_pt hidden true
    label l_qd "50" below-right #b5482e attach_to qd_pt hidden true
    label l_new "$60, 40 crates" left attach_to eq hidden true
    arrow cw1 #d0865f from.x 30 from.y 50 to.x 30 to.y 70 hidden true
    arrow cw2 #d0865f from.x 30 from.y 70 to.x 50 to.y 70 hidden true
    arrow cw3 #d0865f from.x 50 from.y 70 to.x 50 to.y 50 hidden true
    arrow cw4 #d0865f from.x 50 from.y 50 to.x 30 to.y 50 hidden true
    label l_70 "$70" above #d0865f attach_to cw2 hidden true
    region lost x_from 30 x_to.ref eq between ["demand","supply"] style.fill #8f887c hidden true
    region surplus x_from 5 x_to 30 between ["demand","supply"] style.fill #f2c14e hidden true

    card title "How the price finds its way"

When demand shifts, does a market really move to the new equilibrium, and how? Take strawberries at one market: crates a day, and the price of a crate.
    draw ax

Growers bring more crates, the better the price.
    draw supply label_S

Buyers take fewer, the dearer they are.
    draw demand label_D

They meet at fifty dollars and thirty crates, and that is where the market price sits.
    draw eq label_eq pline l_pline

    erase eq label_eq

Then a heat wave: at every price, buyers want twenty more crates, and demand slides right.
    animate s 20 duration 3 ghost ["demand"]

Nobody can see the new crossing. What everyone sees is this: at fifty dollars, buyers want fifty crates and growers bring thirty.
    draw qs_pt l_qs qd_pt l_qd excess l_excess

Stalls sell out by noon, and buyers ask for more. The shortage is the signal.
    point at.ref excess gesture underline

So sellers ask more. Each step up brings more crates in and talks some buyers out of it, until the gap is gone.
    draw climb

It closes at the new crossing: sixty dollars, forty crates. Nobody had to find it; the shortage led there.
    draw eq label_eq l_new

But that assumes growers can answer at once. Strawberries are planted a season ahead, on last season's price.
    erase excess l_excess climb qs_pt qd_pt pline l_pline l_qs l_qd l_new demand_ghost

Fifty dollars brings only thirty crates, so buyers bid them up to seventy. Seventy brings fifty crates, a glut, and the price falls back to fifty.
    draw cw1 l_70 cw2 cw3

And round again. With these curves the price never settles: it jumps between fifty and seventy, circling the sixty it never reaches.
    draw cw4

Arthur Hanau found such swings in German pig prices in 1928. Economists still call it the hog cycle.
    point at.ref cw2 gesture circle

This wedge is what buyers and growers gain from trading. It is largest at the crossing, so every lap of the cycle wastes some of it.
    draw surplus lost

So the equilibrium does pull the price, through shortage and glut. Whether the price arrives depends on how fast supply can answer.
    focus cw1 cw2 cw3 cw4 eq

    quiz "What if growers reacted to price less strongly than buyers?"
        right: The swings die out: each crop overshoots less than the last, so the price spirals in to the crossing.
        + The swings die out
        * The swings grow
        * The swings stay the same
---
